1
00:00:01,008 --> 00:00:03,190
CI is where everything begins.

2
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:06,690
It's the engine that tells you your code compiles,

3
00:00:06,730 --> 00:00:09,789
your tests passed, your container image is secure,

4
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:12,649
and your build artifact is ready to deploy.

5
00:00:12,810 --> 00:00:17,659
And if your CI pipeline breaks, your entire delivery process comes to a halt.

6
00:00:17,850 --> 00:00:20,450
Let's just see how we can build this continuous

7
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:22,049
integration with the cloud build.

8
00:00:22,209 --> 00:00:23,219
Let me create a folder.

9
00:00:23,329 --> 00:00:26,610
Now, let's go inside that folder, and let's create one file.

10
00:00:26,729 --> 00:00:28,530
I'm using an open editor now.

11
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:30,889
Inside this folder, let me create a file,

12
00:00:31,049 --> 00:00:35,680
quickstart.sh, and let's have this Hello, world code inside that.

13
00:00:35,770 --> 00:00:38,979
Let's create one more file that is a Dockerfile.

14
00:00:39,169 --> 00:00:40,569
Now inside my Dockerfile,

15
00:00:40,689 --> 00:00:44,330
I'm just writing this small code saying FROM Alpine, COPY

16
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:48,450
quickstart.sh, and CMD/quickstart.sh.

17
00:00:48,729 --> 00:00:52,930
Now go back to the terminal window, and let me say here chmod and

18
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:55,840
give the execute permission on quickstart.sh.

19
00:00:56,209 --> 00:00:59,009
Now type in the command saying gcloud artifacts

20
00:00:59,009 --> 00:01:02,610
repositories create quickstart‑docker‑repo1,

21
00:01:02,729 --> 00:01:05,830
and you give the location and format as docker.

22
00:01:05,930 --> 00:01:06,690
Let's enter.

23
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:10,449
It will take a while, and then it will create the repository.

24
00:01:10,569 --> 00:01:13,629
Use the gcloud builds submit command to build the

25
00:01:13,629 --> 00:01:15,569
Docker image using a Dockerfile.

26
00:01:15,690 --> 00:01:18,449
You don't require a separate cloud‑built config file.

27
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:22,809
Let's get your cloud project ID by running the following command.

28
00:01:22,970 --> 00:01:28,379
Now use the same project ID and write the command gcloud builds submit.

29
00:01:28,779 --> 00:01:29,669
Give the region,

30
00:01:30,099 --> 00:01:35,199
give tag which contains your project ID and entire repo information,

31
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,139
and say Enter.

32
00:01:36,309 --> 00:01:39,059
Now you can see that it has started the build.

33
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:42,239
Now it's pushing the repository image,

34
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:44,779
and then you can see that it is successful.

35
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:51,739
So now what we can do is we can go to our UI and search for cloud build.

36
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:58,220
And there we should be able to see the build that we have generated just now.

37
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:01,569
You can see that we got this one build ready.

38
00:02:01,730 --> 00:02:05,720
You can click on that build and you can see the build ID.

39
00:02:05,839 --> 00:02:11,139
If you come down to our console, you can see the ID here, f53,

40
00:02:11,309 --> 00:02:14,949
which exactly is the same you can see here at the top.

41
00:02:15,070 --> 00:02:19,199
So this is the specific build which we have created.

42
00:02:19,309 --> 00:02:22,110
If you want to go inside the build artifacts,

43
00:02:22,229 --> 00:02:24,820
you can see the name of the image as well.

44
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:29,190
Later on, if you want to see the repository, you can see the repositories here.

45
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:32,740
And if you want to add the triggers, for example,

46
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,960
you want this build to get automatically generated whenever

47
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:41,880
some triggers happens up like trigger could be when somebody

48
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:46,880
pushes something inside the repository or maybe someone pushes

49
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:49,009
a new tag or a pull request,

50
00:02:49,279 --> 00:02:53,279
and in that case, you automatically wanted to create the trigger,

51
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:57,690
you can just set that trigger here, and that will run the build for you.

52
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:00,259
You can make this build automated.
